## Streaming Large Uploads in ASP.NET Core 8 Minimal API

`IFormFile` buffers the entire request into memory by default. For large files, read directly from the `Request.Body` (or use `MultipartReader`) to stream to disk in chunks.

### Key steps:

**1. Disable request size limits:**

```csharp
// Program.cs
builder.WebApplication.MapPost("/upload", HandleUpload)
    .DisableAntiforgery()
    .WithMetadata(new DisableRequestSizeLimitAttribute());

// Also in Program.cs (Kestrel config):
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(options =>
{
    options.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 2 GB
});
```

**2. Stream multipart sections to disk with `MultipartReader`:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities;
using Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers;

async Task<IResult> HandleUpload(HttpRequest request)
{
    if (!request.HasFormContentType ||
        !MediaTypeHeaderValue.TryParse(request.ContentType, out var mt) ||
        string.IsNullOrEmpty(mt.Boundary.Value))
    {
        return Results.BadRequest("Expected a multipart request.");
    }

    var boundary = HeaderUtilities.RemoveQuotes(mt.Boundary).Value!;
    var reader = new MultipartReader(boundary, request.Body);

    MultipartSection? section;
    while ((section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync()) != null)
    {
        if (!ContentDispositionHeaderValue.TryParse(section.ContentDisposition, out var cd) ||
            !cd.IsFileDisposition())
        {
            continue; // skip non-file fields
        }

        var trustedName = Path.GetRandomFileName(); // avoid user-supplied names
        var savePath = Path.Combine(Path.GetTempPath(), trustedName);

        await using var targetStream = File.Create(savePath);
        await section.Body.CopyToAsync(targetStream);

        return Results.Ok(new { savedAs = trustedName, size = targetStream.Length });
    }

    return Results.BadRequest("No file found in request.");
}
```

### Why this works

| Concern | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| Memory pressure | `CopyToAsync` streams in 81 KB internal buffer chunks — never holds the full file |
| Kestrel size gate | `MaxRequestBodySize` raised to 2 GB |
| Form model-binding | Bypassed entirely — no `IFormFile`, no `IFormFileCollection` |
| Security | `Path.GetRandomFileName()` avoids path traversal from user filenames |

### If behind IIS/reverse proxy

Add to `web.config`:

```xml
<system.webServer>
  <security>
    <requestFiltering>
      <requestLimits maxAllowedContentLength="2147483648" />
    </requestFiltering>
  </security>
</system.webServer>
```

### Optional: progress / validation

- To cap individual file size, count bytes manually in a copy loop instead of `CopyToAsync`.
- To validate file type, inspect the first few bytes (magic numbers) after writing, then delete if invalid.
